    <DataNode elementId="INDOLE_PYRUVATE" textLabel="(indol-3-yl)pyruvate" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="RSTKLPZEZYGQPY-UHFFFAOYSA-M" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="500" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, nilo-poyanco">There is evidence that this compound exists in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[9561757]| and in |FRAME: TAX-4081| (originally called |FRAME:TAX-4081| (L.) Mill.)|CITS:[COONEY91]|. Another study suggests that indole-3-pyruvate (IPA) is widely distributed in the plant kingdom, based on the ability of enzymatic extracts from 30 different species distributed among 16 families, to catalyze the formation of this compound |CITS:[TRUELSEN72]|. At least three different species of algae also appear to have enzymes capable of producing IPA |CITS:[TRUELSEN72]|.</Comment>
